# Presentation

Presenting a result has three parts, each with its own mechanism. A **shape
type** is what the value is: `Graph` and `Table` are ordinary types, built by
their constructors. A **format** is the syntax it is written in: a value of
`Format`, a category of its own, which describes an external representation
and its framing and is not the text it encodes. An **encoder** writes values
of one type in one format; formatting applies the encoder for the value's
type, and the result is a `String` a later step may read, which keeps its
format for presentation. A **renderer** is a named encoder for one shape in
one format, chosen per call with `renderer =` or per vault in `hql.toml`. A
result a program leaves unformatted is formatted in the host's **presentation
context**.

This card is the statement of the presentation model.
[HQL-0017](../../proposals/HQL-0017/README.md) specifies the shapes, the
renderers and the context, and
[HQL-0024 part 7](../../proposals/HQL-0024/parts/07-formats-and-codecs.md)
the format and codec model; both point here for the current rules.

## Shape types

| Type | Declared | Built by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Graph` | `std/graph.hql` | `Graph(xs)`: cards, hits or edges as nodes, the links among them as edges |
| `Table` | `std/format.hql` | `Table(xs, columns = [...])`: one row per record |
| `Data` | `std/core.hql` | a literal, a read, or `Data(s, F)`, text parsed in a format |
| `Doc` | `std/doc.hql` | the vault |

`Tree<T>`, a node and its subtrees, is design direction and not implemented.

### `Table`

```hql
fn Table<T>(rows: Collection<T>, columns: List<String> = []) : Table
```

`Table(xs, columns = [...])` builds a `Table` from a finite collection: one
row per element, each a value of a declared record type or a `Data` map.

- `columns` names the columns in the order every format writes them. Without
  it, the columns are the first row's fields in the order the row holds them:
  declaration order for a record, key order for a `Data` map.
- Over a declared record type, a column written in `columns` that names no
  field of the record is a type error when the program is checked.
- Over `Data`, a field a row does not hold is an empty cell. An absent
  optional field of a record is an empty cell too.
- An element type that is neither a record nor `Data`, such as `Int`, is a
  type error; `map` each element to a record first. A stream that may not
  end is refused, naming `take(n)` and `collect(n)`.
- `table` is not a step: written as one, it is a name error whose diagnostic
  names `Table(xs, columns = [...])`.

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
| map(c => {title: c.title, pages: c.metadata.pages})
| Table(columns = ["title", "pages"])
| markdown
```

A `Table` printed as HQL is the call that builds it,
`Table([...], columns = [...])`.

## Presentation formats

| Format | Supertype | Representation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Text` | `Format` | plain text |
| `Lines` | `Format` | text framed one record per line |
| `Hql` | `Format` | HQL literals |
| `Markdown` | `Format` | CommonMark |
| `Hmd` | `Markdown` | HyperMarkDown |
| `Json` | `Format` | one JSON document |
| `JsonLines` | `Format` | one JSON document per line |
| `Yaml` | `Format` | YAML |
| `Csv` | `Format` | RFC 4180 rows |
| `D2` | `Format` | D2 diagram source |
| `Mermaid` | `Format` | Mermaid diagram source |

A format is a value of `Format`, a category of its own: it describes an
external representation and its framing, and is neither the text it encodes
nor a `String` subtype. `format(x, F)` applies the encoder for `x`'s type in
`F` and gives the encoded text as a `String`, which keeps its format at run
time for a host to print as it is. `source(e, F)` applies the decoder.
The contracts are the traits `Decoder<T>` and `Encoder<T>` in
`std/format.hql`, described under [codecs](#codecs), and the model is
[HQL-0024 part 7](../../proposals/HQL-0024/parts/07-formats-and-codecs.md).

## Codecs

A **codec** is the conversion between a format's text and HQL values: a
**decoder** reads records of the format as values, an **encoder** writes
values as records of the format. A codec is declared in HQL and runs in
Rust.

### What is declared

`std/format.hql` declares two traits whose implementing type is the format,
and one bodyless header per codec the runtime has:

```hql
trait Decoder<T> {}
trait Encoder<T> {}

impl Decoder<String> for Lines
impl<T> Encoder<T> for Lines
```

`impl Decoder<String> for Lines` states that the records of `Lines` read as
`String` values; `impl<T> Encoder<T> for Lines` states that a value of every
type is written as `Lines` records. The traits have no functions and the
headers no bodies: a header converts nothing. It is the declaration of a
conversion that exists, in the language rather than in a table of the
binary, and the conversion itself is performed by the `io` and `format`
extensions when a program calls `source`, `tap`, `sink`, `target` or
`format`. These are generic traits, described on [[type-system]]
([HQL-0027](../../proposals/HQL-0027/README.md)).

### What runs: `Lines`, end to end

```hql
source(file("birds.txt"), Lines)
| map(name => upper(name))
| sink(file("loud.txt"), Lines)
```

Over a `birds.txt` holding `raven\nrobin\n`, the steps are these.

1. **Binding**, once per runtime environment, before any program is
   checked. `validate_codecs` in `src/extensions/binding.rs` compares the headers of
   `std/format.hql` with the runtime's two tables: `io::document_type`, the
   decoder table, maps `Lines` to `String`, which is what the one `Decoder`
   header for `Lines` names; `format::writable`, the encoder table, accepts
   every type for `Lines`, which is what `impl<T> Encoder<T> for Lines`
   covers. A disagreement in either direction is a defect of the binary and
   fails binding, naming the format and the type.
2. **Checking.** The `io` extension's check for `source` reads the format
   argument, `Lines`, and gives the element type from the decoder table:
   `String`. A file is finite, so the result is `Seq<String>`. `map` then
   types `name` as `String`. `sink` writes one record per value, so its check
   refuses a format that describes a whole, `D2` or `Mermaid`; `format(x, F)`
   consults the encoder table, and a type `F` does not write is a type error
   naming both.
3. **Reading.** `eval_source` calls `io::read_all`, which opens the file and
   hands the byte stream to `io::pieces` with the format. For `Lines` that is
   a buffered reader split at each line terminator, `\n` or `\r\n`: each line,
   without its terminator, becomes one `Value::Str`. The terminator belongs to
   the representation, so the values are `"raven"` and `"robin"`, not
   `"raven\n"`. A final line without a terminator is still a record.
4. **Writing.** `eval_sink` opens the destination once, then for each value
   calls `format::piece(value, Lines)` for the record's text and writes it
   followed by a terminator: `RAVEN\nROBIN\n`. A `String` is written as its
   characters; any other value as its own rendering in `Text`, one value per
   line.

`Json` follows the same route with a different reader and writer: `pieces`
runs a streaming JSON deserializer and turns each document into a `Data`
tree, and `piece` serializes the value's data. `std/format.hql` is the
statement of which routes exist; `src/io.rs` and `src/extensions/format.rs`
are the routes.

### Decoding and encoding are not symmetric

A decoder and an encoder are declared apart because a format does not read
and write the same types:

| Format | Decodes as | Encodes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Json`, `JsonLines`, `Yaml`, `Csv` | `Data` | every type |
| `Text`, `Lines`, `Markdown`, `Hmd`, `Hql` | `String` | every type |
| `D2`, `Mermaid` | nothing | `Graph`, and `Data` whose value is a graph |

- **Encoding is many to one.** Every value has a rendering in a text format:
  a `Person`, a `List<Int>` and a `Graph` all have JSON text. One header with
  a type parameter, `impl<T> Encoder<T> for Json`, covers them.
- **Decoding is one to one.** The text of a JSON document does not record the
  type of the value it was written from, so reading it gives the open tree,
  `Data`, and a `Person` is obtained from that tree by
  [[type-interpretation|refinement]], not by the decoder. The header names
  exactly one type, `impl Decoder<Data> for Json`. A read whose expected
  type names a record, `people : Seq<Person> = source(file("people.json"))`,
  applies that refinement to each document as it is read; a CSV cell, which
  is text, is parsed as the scalar its field declares.
- **Some formats are written only.** `D2` and `Mermaid` are diagram source
  for a viewer; no decoder reads them back, so they have encoders and no
  `Decoder` header.

A single trait for both directions would state one set of types for both,
which holds for none of these formats: `Json` would claim to read every type
or to write only `Data`, and `D2` would claim to be read. Two traits let each
direction state its own coverage, and binding checks each against its own
table.

## `format` and its shorthands

```hql
fn format<T, F : Format>(x: T, as: Type<F>, *, limit: Int = 0, renderer: String = "") : String
```

`format` is pure and not terminal: its result type is the format, so a step
after it is checked like any other.

- `limit`, when positive, keeps the first `limit` top-level elements — items
  of a list, hits of a ranking, nodes of a graph, rows of a table — and the
  text marks how many were cut.
- `renderer` names the renderer for this call, over the configured one. A
  renderer for a format other than `F` is a type error, and so is a table
  renderer over a value whose checked type is not `Table`.
- A type and format no renderer writes is a check error naming both. `D2` and
  `Mermaid` describe graphs, so only a `Graph` is written in them.
- Over text already in a format `F`: `format(F)` and `format(Text)` are the
  identity, `Markdown` and `Hmd` widen into each other and copy nothing, and
  any other format written as `Markdown` or `Hmd` is a fenced block with that
  format's info string. No other conversion between formats exists.

Each shorthand fixes the format and nothing else; an option is written in the
longhand, `format(Markdown, limit = 50)`.

| Shorthand | Longhand |
| --- | --- |
| `text` | `format(Text)` |
| `hql` | `format(Hql)` |
| `markdown` | `format(Markdown)` |
| `hmd` | `format(Hmd)` |
| `json` | `format(Json)` |
| `yaml` | `format(Yaml)` |
| `csv` | `format(Csv)` |
| `d2` | `format(D2)` |
| `mermaid` | `format(Mermaid)` |

```hql
cards
| Graph
| format(Markdown, limit = 20)
```

## Renderers

| Renderer | Type | Formats | Writes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `adjacency` | `Graph` | `Text` | one line per node, its edges indented |
| `node-link` | `Graph` | `Json` | the Cytoscape elements form: `nodes` and `edges`, each under `data` |
| `d2` | `Graph` | `D2`, `Markdown`, `Hmd` | D2 source; in `Markdown`, fenced as `d2` |
| `mermaid` | `Graph` | `Mermaid`, `Markdown`, `Hmd` | a Mermaid `graph` block; in `Markdown`, fenced as `mermaid` |
| `aligned` | `Table` | `Text` | space-aligned columns under a dashed rule |
| `pipe` | `Table` | `Markdown`, `Hmd` | a pipe table with header and delimiter rows |
| `rows` | `Table` | `Csv` | RFC 4180 rows, header first |
| `records` | `Table` | `Json` | an array of objects, keys in column order |
| `list` | any | `Markdown`, `Hmd` | the value's own Markdown rendering |

A graph renderer writes only a `Graph` and a table renderer only a `Table`;
naming one for another value is refused. A `Graph` defaults to `adjacency` in
`Text`, `node-link` in `Json`, `d2` in `Markdown`, `Hmd` and `D2`, and
`mermaid` in `Mermaid`.

A `Table` is written by each format's own encoder for `Table`, with no
renderer named, and every one of them writes the columns in the table's
order:

| Format | A `Table` written |
| --- | --- |
| `Text`, `Lines` | space-aligned columns under a dashed rule, as `aligned` |
| `Markdown`, `Hmd` | a pipe table, as `pipe` |
| `Csv` | RFC 4180 rows, header first, as `rows` |
| `Json` | an array of objects, as `records`; a missing cell is `null` |
| `JsonLines` | one object per row, per line |
| `Yaml` | a sequence of mappings; a cell that is not a scalar in flow style |
| `Hql` | the call that builds it |

Any other value is written in `Text` and `Markdown` as its own rendering, in
`Json` and `Yaml` as its data, and in `Csv` as rows.

A value in `Hql` is the literal that writes it, so the text reads back as the
same value: a string in quotes, a list in square brackets, a set in braces —
`{"novel", "essay"}`, or `Set<Int>([])` when it is empty, since `{}` is an
empty record. A cut is marked with a `//` comment. Text of another format
written as `Hql` is a string literal. A value's own rendering in `Text` is the
same with one exception: a string standing alone is written bare.

### The vault's `[format]` configuration

A vault chooses a renderer per type and format in `hql.toml`, one table per
type, mapping a format's lowercase name to a renderer. A value whose type has
no entry takes the entry of its nearest supertype, then the default:

```toml
[format.Graph]
markdown = "mermaid"

[format.Table]
json = "records"
```

A renderer or format the binary does not have, a renderer named for a format
it does not write, or a graph or table renderer named for another type fails
loading the vault, before any program runs.

## The presentation context

A host writes a result that is not already formatted in a presentation
context, as if the program ended in `| format(<context>)`. The context is,
first match wins, a final `format` step in the program, then `--to <format>`
on the command line, then the host's own: `hmd` for `hql render`, `hql` for
`hql eval`, `hql run` and the REPL. A result already formatted is written as
it is: in a document, `Markdown` spliced in and any other format fenced with
its info string. `--to none` evaluates the program and writes no value.
[[hql/knowledge|Knowledge]] has no default, because its views are equally
valid and picking one silently would make a presentation choice look like a
property of the knowledge.

### Streams

Over a stream that may not end, `format` gives a `Stream<String>`: one piece
per value, written as the value arrives — a line in `Text`, a list item in
`Markdown`, a row in `Csv` with the header first, a value per line in `Json`,
a `---` document in `Yaml`. `D2` and `Mermaid` describe a whole, so a stream
is refused there until `take(n)` or `collect(n)` bounds it. A host writes a
stream result piece by piece as it arrives, and the stream ends when its
reader stops reading.

Rendering belongs to the host. Printing or saving is an explicit effect, and
constructing an updated [[card-type|Card]] does not itself persist a document.

See [[pipes]] for the execution direction and [[hql/knowledge]] for the
projection this separates itself from.
